/* Lesson quizzes — batch 18 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p51m1":[
 {id:"p51m1q1", q:"A 25-second job spends 20 s in one loop. You port that loop to the GPU, where it runs 50× faster, and leave the other 5 s of loading, preprocessing and copying alone. What is the new overall speedup, and the most any faster kernel could ever give?", options:["About 40×; the ceiling is 50×","About 4.6×; the ceiling is 5×","About 10×; the ceiling is 25×","About 4.6×; the ceiling is 50×"], answer:1, why:"Only the loop speeds up: 5 + 20/50 = 5.4 s, so 25 ÷ 5.4 ≈ 4.6×. The untouched 5 s is a serial fraction of 0.2, so even an infinitely fast kernel gives 1/0.2 = 5×. The kernel's 50× is not the program's speedup."},
 {id:"p51m1q2", q:"A code measures speedups of 3.5× on 4 threads, 8× on 16 threads and 9× on 24 threads. What does the Karp–Flatt metric suggest about what limits it?", options:["e stays near 0.05, so a fixed block of serial code is the limit","e falls as N grows, so cache effects are making it superlinear","e is about 0.63 at 24 threads, so most of the code is serial","e rises from about 0.05 to 0.07, so overhead is growing with N"], answer:3, why:"e = (1/S − 1/N)/(1 − 1/N) gives 0.048, 0.067 and 0.072. A climbing e means a cost that grows with thread count, such as synchronization, communication or imbalance; a flat e would point to genuinely serial code. 0.63 is one minus the efficiency, not e."},
 {id:"p51m1q3", q:"A memory system delivers 120 GB/s with 100 ns of latency. By Little's law, how much data must be in flight at every moment to sustain that bandwidth?", options:["About 12 KB, roughly 190 cache lines","About 1.2 KB, roughly 19 cache lines","About 120 KB, roughly 1,900 cache lines","One cache line, if the prefetcher is fast enough"], answer:0, why:"Bytes in flight = bandwidth × latency = 120 × 10⁹ B/s × 100 × 10⁻⁹ s = 12,000 bytes, about 188 lines of 64 bytes. One line at a time would deliver only 64 bytes per 100 ns, 0.64 GB/s; that is why prefetchers, out-of-order cores and many threads exist."},
 {id:"p51m1q4", q:"A 4-bit LLM decoding at batch 1 has an arithmetic intensity of about 4 FLOP/B on the RTX 5090 Laptop GPU, whose ridge is about 50 FLOP/B. Roughly how many requests must be batched before the weight-reading matrix multiplies become compute-bound?", options:["About 4 requests","About 50 requests","About 13 requests","None; decoding is memory-bound at any batch size"], answer:2, why:"Each weight byte read now feeds every request in the batch, so intensity grows roughly linearly, I ≈ 4 × B, and crosses the 50 FLOP/B ridge near B = 13. Decoding is memory-bound only at small batch; batching moves the kernel to the right along the roofline."},
 {id:"p51m1q5", q:"Your team reports 92% efficiency on 512 GPUs for data-parallel training with a fixed batch per GPU, so the global batch grows with the GPU count. A reviewer objects that the 5% serial part caps speedup at 20× by Amdahl's law. Who is right?", options:["The reviewer: Amdahl's law bounds every parallel run, so 92% is a measurement error","The team: work per GPU is fixed, so this is weak scaling and Gustafson's law applies","Both, but only while the job uses fewer than 20 GPUs","Neither: data-parallel training has no serial part at all"], answer:1, why:"Amdahl fixes the problem size. Here the problem grows with the machine, so the serial 5% stays small next to the growing parallel work and the scaled speedup is about N − s(N − 1). Both laws are correct; they answer different questions."}
],
"p51m2":[
 {id:"p51m2q1", q:"What is wrong with this OpenMP loop?\n double t;\n #pragma omp parallel for\n for (long i = 0; i < n; i++) { t = a[i] * 2.0; b[i] = t + 1.0; }", options:["The index i is shared, so iterations get skipped; add private(i)","Writes to b[i] race between threads; wrap them in omp critical","t is shared by default, so threads clobber each other's value; make t private","Nothing: OpenMP makes every variable used inside the loop private"], answer:2, why:"Variables declared outside the region are shared by default; only the loop index is made private automatically. Another thread can overwrite t between the two statements. Each thread writes different b[i], so that part is not a race. default(none) would have caught the bug."},
 {id:"p51m2q2", q:"A chip has 6 P-cores and 8 E-cores, and on your uniform loop an E-core does half the work of a P-core. Roughly what speedup over one P-core do schedule(static) and schedule(dynamic) give on 14 threads?", options:["Static about 7×; dynamic approaches about 10×","Both about 14×, since every thread gets equal work","Static about 10×; dynamic approaches about 14×","Static about 7×; dynamic stays near 7× as E-cores are slow"], answer:0, why:"Static gives every thread W/14, and the E-cores need twice as long, finishing at W/7: 7×. Dynamic lets fast cores take more chunks, approaching the true capacity of 6 + 8 × 0.5 = 10 P-core equivalents. The slowest thread sets a parallel region's time."},
 {id:"p51m2q3", q:"On a two-socket server, a 32 GB array is filled by a serial loop and then processed by a parallel for loop whose threads are spread across both sockets. The compute loop reaches only about half the expected bandwidth. What is the fix?", options:["Switch the compute loop to schedule(dynamic) so idle threads steal work","Pad every element of the array to 64 bytes to stop false sharing","Run more threads than cores so that extra threads hide remote latency","Fill the array in parallel with the same static schedule and pinning"], answer:3, why:"Under first touch, Linux puts each page on the node of the thread that first writes it, so the serial fill placed all 32 GB on one socket and half the threads read across the link. Dynamic scheduling makes it worse, since iterations no longer match the pages each thread touched."},
 {id:"p51m2q4", q:"Your OpenMP build takes 10.0 s with OMP_NUM_THREADS=1 and 0.90 s with 24 threads. The best serial version, compiled with the same flags but without OpenMP, takes 7.2 s. What speedup should you report?", options:["About 11.1×","About 8.0×","About 24×","About 1.4×"], answer:1, why:"Speedup is measured against the best serial program: 7.2 ÷ 0.90 = 8.0×. Dividing by the 10 s one-thread OpenMP run gives 11.1×, which credits you for removing your own parallel overhead and inflates the result."},
 {id:"p51m2q5", q:"Your parallel sum with reduction(+:sum) prints 3.141592653589 on 8 threads and 3.141592653592 on 24. ThreadSanitizer with LLVM's Archer runtime reports nothing. What is going on?", options:["A race on sum that only shows up at higher thread counts","Turbo clocks change the rounding mode of the floating-point unit","Floating-point addition is not associative, and the order changed","The reduction drops one partial sum when threads outnumber cores"], answer:2, why:"A reduction combines per-thread partial sums in an order that depends on the thread count, and FP addition is not associative, so the last digits move. A real race gives large, random errors and TSan flags it. Fix the thread count for regression tests or use Kahan summation."}
],
"p51m3":[
 {id:"p51m3q1", q:"Profiling shows that threads spend 8% of their run time inside one global mutex that guards a hash table. What is the best possible speedup, however many threads you add, and which change attacks that limit?", options:["12.5×; swap the mutex for a spinlock so that handoffs get faster","24×; each thread holds the lock for only 8% of its own time","About 22×; 24 threads × 92% is the parallel capacity left","12.5×; stripe the lock across bucket groups so threads rarely meet"], answer:3, why:"Time inside a single critical section is serial fraction: 1/0.08 = 12.5× at most, and queues form before that. A spinlock only speeds the handoff; the section still runs one thread at a time. Lock striping lets threads touching different buckets proceed in parallel."},
 {id:"p51m3q2", q:"A CUDA kernel has each of 4 million threads call atomicAdd(&total, x) on one global variable, and it is slow. Which redesign follows the lesson's synchronization hierarchy?", options:["Warp shuffles, then one shared atomic per warp and one global atomic per block","Plain total += x instead, since a warp's 32 threads run in lockstep anyway","A __syncthreads() before each atomicAdd, so each block's updates queue in order","A __threadfence() after each atomicAdd, so the L2 cache resolves them faster"], answer:0, why:"Every global atomic on one address serializes at L2. Shuffles sum 32 values in five steps with no memory traffic, and the per-warp and per-block atomics cut global contention by about a thousand times. Plain += is a race even within a warp, and fences add ordering, not speed."},
 {id:"p51m3q3", q:"A 256-thread block runs: if (threadIdx.x < 128) { load_tile(); __syncthreads(); } compute(); The kernel sometimes hangs and sometimes gives garbage. Why?", options:["__syncthreads() waits for every block in the grid, and some are not resident","Threads 128–255 never reach the barrier, a mismatch that can hang the block","Barriers only work correctly in blocks of 32 threads or fewer","The barrier belongs after compute(), because it orders writes, not reads"], answer:1, why:"__syncthreads() is a block-level barrier that every thread of the block must reach. Inside a branch that only half the threads take, it is a barrier mismatch, like an OpenMP barrier inside an if. It never spans the grid; the kernel boundary is the grid-wide barrier."},
 {id:"p51m3q4", q:"Twenty-four threads count events with atomic_fetch_add on one shared _Atomic long, and the total is read only after all threads are joined. Which memory order is sufficient, and what then limits throughput?", options:["seq_cst, as weaker orders can lose increments; fence cost limits it","acquire on every add, so later adds see earlier ones; the store buffer limits it","relaxed, as the join orders the final read; the one shared cache line limits it","None needed: a volatile long gives the same total without atomic cost"], answer:2, why:"Atomicity alone prevents lost increments, so relaxed suffices for a pure counter and the thread join makes the total visible. Every add still needs exclusive ownership of one cache line, so the threads serialize on it; accumulating locally is faster. volatile gives no atomicity and loses updates."},
 {id:"p51m3q5", q:"A high-priority control thread misses deadlines. Traces show it waiting on a mutex held by a low-priority logging thread, which rarely runs because a medium-priority thread keeps the CPU busy. What is this, and what is the standard fix?", options:["Priority inversion; enable priority inheritance on the mutex","Deadlock; impose one global lock order to break circular wait","Livelock; add randomized back-off to the retry loop","Lock contention; split the mutex into several striped locks"], answer:0, why:"The high-priority thread is stuck behind a low-priority holder that a medium-priority thread starves: priority inversion, the bug that reset Mars Pathfinder. Priority inheritance temporarily raises the holder so it can finish and release the lock. There is no cycle of waiting, so it is not deadlock."}
],
"p51m4":[
 {id:"p51m4q1", q:"On a link with α = 2 µs and β = 25 GB/s, compare sending 4,000 messages of 1,000 bytes each with sending one message of 4,000,000 bytes.", options:["Both take about 160 µs, since the total bytes are equal","About 0.16 ms for the small messages, 8.2 ms for the big one","About 8.2 ms for the small messages, 0.16 ms for the big one","About 8 ms for the small messages, 4 ms for the big one"], answer:2, why:"Each message pays α: 4,000 × (2 + 0.04) µs ≈ 8.2 ms, almost all latency. The single message pays α once plus 4 MB ÷ 25 GB/s = 160 µs. Below the half-performance size α × β = 50 KB, merging small messages into large ones wins."},
 {id:"p51m4q2", q:"Two MPI ranks each call MPI_Send with 64 MB for the other and then MPI_Recv. With a 1 KB test input it works; at full size it hangs. Why, and what is the fix?", options:["The network drops large messages; resend with an acknowledgment loop","Large sends use rendezvous and wait for a receive; use MPI_Sendrecv","MPI_Send returns only after delivery; add MPI_Barrier before the sends","Small sends must use MPI_Isend; large ones are safe with blocking calls"], answer:1, why:"Small messages are buffered (eager), so MPI_Send returns at once; large ones wait until the receiver posts a matching receive. Both ranks sit in send, so neither reaches its receive. MPI_Sendrecv, or Isend/Irecv with Waitall, removes the circular wait; a barrier changes nothing."},
 {id:"p51m4q3", q:"A 16384 × 16384 Jacobi grid is split into square 2D blocks, one per rank. You strong-scale from 16 ranks to 1,024. By roughly what factor does each rank's halo-to-compute ratio change?", options:["It stays the same, as halo and compute both shrink with p","It rises about 64×, as each block is 64 times smaller","It falls about 8×, as the halo messages get 8 times shorter","It rises about 8×: compute falls 64× but the halo only 8×"], answer:3, why:"Compute scales with a block's area, n²/p, which falls 64×; the halo scales with its perimeter, n/√p, which falls only √64 = 8×. Communication grows 8× relative to work, and strong scaling ends when halo time approaches compute time."},
 {id:"p51m4q4", q:"Each node in a cluster fails on average once every 10 years, about 87,600 hours. Roughly how often does a 2,000-node machine lose a node, and what does that mean for a job spanning all of it?", options:["About every 44 hours; the job must checkpoint well inside that","About every 10 years; failures are independent, so nothing changes","About every 4.4 hours; the job should restart every 4 hours","About every 438 hours; checkpoints are needed only for month-long runs"], answer:0, why:"With independent failures the rates add: one failure every 87,600 ÷ 2,000 ≈ 44 hours. A tightly coupled MPI job usually dies with any of its nodes, so it checkpoints at an interval set by Young's formula from that MTBF. 4.4 hours would be 20,000 nodes."},
 {id:"p51m4q5", q:"Why does GPUDirect RDMA speed up sending a buffer from a GPU on one node to a GPU on another?", options:["It compresses the buffer on the GPU before it crosses the network","It stretches a PCIe link between the two nodes in place of the network","The NIC reads and writes GPU memory directly, skipping host RAM","It lets the remote CPU inspect each packet before the GPU sees it"], answer:2, why:"RDMA lets the network card access registered memory without the remote CPU or either kernel on the data path; GPUDirect extends that to GPU memory, so data no longer bounces GPU → host RAM → NIC. The remote CPU is bypassed, not added, and the link is still the network."}
],
"p51m5":[
 {id:"p51m5q1", q:"A kernel uses 128-thread blocks, 40 registers per thread and 24 KB of shared memory per block. On compute capability 12.0 (48 warps, 64K registers and 128 KB of shared memory per SM), what is its theoretical occupancy?", options:["100%, set by the 48-warp limit","About 42%, set by shared memory","About 50%, set by the register file","About 25%, set by the 32-block limit"], answer:1, why:"Registers allow 65,536 ÷ (128 × 40) = 12 blocks and warps allow 48 ÷ 4 = 12, but shared memory allows only 128 ÷ 24 = 5 blocks. Five blocks of 4 warps is 20 of 48 warps, about 42%. The scarcest resource sets occupancy."},
 {id:"p51m5q2", q:"Two versions of a kernel differ in one line: version A branches on threadIdx.x % 2, version B on (threadIdx.x / 32) % 2. Both paths cost the same. How do they compare?", options:["A is faster: alternating lanes balance the work in each warp","Both run the same, since each thread has its own program counter","B is slower: whole warps idle while the other half of the block works","B is faster: each warp takes one path, while A's warps run both"], answer:3, why:"A warp issues one instruction for all 32 lanes, so when lanes disagree it runs both paths in turn with half the lanes masked, halving throughput. B's condition is the same for every lane of a warp. Independent program counters make divergence correct, not free."},
 {id:"p51m5q3", q:"Your FP32 matrix-multiply kernel on the RTX 5090 Laptop GPU reaches 30 TFLOP/s, yet the spec sheet quotes 1,824 AI TOPS. What would let a matrix workload move toward the tensor-core figure?", options:["Narrow inputs such as BF16 or FP8 through cuBLAS, with shapes in multiples of 16","Higher occupancy, so that the CUDA cores issue an FMA every single cycle","FP64 inputs, which GeForce tensor cores accelerate at full rate for HPC","Compiling with -arch=sm_120, which maps every FP32 loop onto tensor cores"], answer:0, why:"The headline is a low-precision tensor-core rate. Tensor cores need supported narrow formats, tile-friendly shapes and libraries that drive them; a plain FP32 loop on the CUDA cores peaks near 45 TFLOP/s whatever its occupancy. GeForce keeps FP64 at a small fraction of FP32."},
 {id:"p51m5q4", q:"A 32B-parameter model quantized to 4-bit is about 18 GB of weights. Ignoring the KV cache, what is its batch-1 decode ceiling on the RTX 5090 Laptop GPU (896 GB/s)?", options:["About 2,500 tokens/s, from the 45 TFLOP/s FP32 rate","About 3.5 tokens/s, because PCIe limits the reads","About 50 tokens/s","About 67 tokens/s"], answer:2, why:"Each token reads every weight once, so the ceiling is bandwidth ÷ bytes per token: 896 ÷ 18 ≈ 50 tokens/s. The 18 GB fits in 24 GB of VRAM, so PCIe is not involved, and decode is memory-bound, so FLOPs do not set it. 67 is the M5 Ultra's figure."},
 {id:"p51m5q5", q:"Capping a kernel at 64 registers with __launch_bounds__ raised occupancy from 50% to 67%, but -Xptxas -v now reports spills and measured DRAM throughput fell. What should you conclude?", options:["The drop is noise; higher occupancy always hides more latency","Spills went to shared memory and caused bank conflicts; pad it","The extra blocks thrash the L2 cache; shrink the grid instead","Spills go to DRAM-backed local memory and cost more; revert"], answer:3, why:"Local memory is per-thread storage in DRAM that is merely cached, so spilled registers add traffic to a kernel already near its bandwidth limit. Occupancy only needs to keep enough bytes in flight; here the extra warps cost more than they hide, so revert."}
],
"p51m6":[
 {id:"p51m6q1", q:"A warp reads only the x field of an array of structs {float x, y, z;} (12 bytes each), one struct per thread. What is the sector efficiency of that load, and the usual fix?", options:["About 33%; store x, y and z as separate arrays","100%; each thread still reads one 4-byte float","12.5%; every thread lands in its own sector","25%; pad each struct to 16 bytes to align it"], answer:0, why:"Thirty-two threads at a 12-byte stride span 384 bytes, twelve 32-byte sectors, for 128 useful bytes: one third. A struct of arrays makes the x values consecutive, so the warp needs only four sectors. Padding to 16 bytes would make it worse, at 25%."},
 {id:"p51m6q2", q:"A job uploads for 30 ms, computes for 30 ms and downloads for 30 ms. You split it into four chunks on four streams with pinned memory, on a GPU with separate upload and download copy engines. About how long does it take?", options:["About 90 ms; copies cannot overlap with kernels","About 30 ms; the three stages run fully in parallel","About 45 ms","About 22.5 ms; four streams make it four times faster"], answer:2, why:"Each chunk's stages take 7.5 ms and the pipeline must fill and drain: (3 stages + 4 chunks − 1) × 7.5 = 45 ms, against 90 ms in sequence. Reaching 30 ms would need infinitely many chunks; pinned memory is what lets the copies run asynchronously."},
 {id:"p51m6q3", q:"A colleague times a grid-stride SAXPY kernel on 2²⁸ floats like this and reports 0.01 ms:\n t0 = now(); saxpy<<<1312, 256>>>(n, 2.0f, x, y); t1 = now();\nThe bandwidth floor is about 3.4 ms. What is wrong?", options:["The grid is too small, so most elements were skipped","The L2 cache held the arrays, so no DRAM traffic occurred","Tensor cores ran the SAXPY, which is how it beat the floor","The launch returns at once, so the timer stopped too early"], answer:3, why:"Kernel launches are asynchronous, so the CPU clock measured only the launch call. Record CUDA events around the kernel, or call cudaDeviceSynchronize() before stopping the timer. A grid-stride loop covers every element whatever the grid size, and 3 GB cannot fit in a 64 MB L2."},
 {id:"p51m6q4", q:"Nsight Compute reports your kernel's duration as 9.1 ms, while CUDA events around the same launch in a normal run measure 3.6 ms. Which number belongs in your write-up?", options:["9.1 ms, because ncu reads the hardware counters directly","3.6 ms, because ncu replays and serializes the kernel","9.1 ms, because events miss the time spent waiting on DRAM","Their average, since each tool is biased in one direction"], answer:1, why:"Nsight Compute replays each kernel many times and serializes work to collect counters, which inflates duration; use it for the reasons (throughput, occupancy, conflicts) and take timings from CUDA events or Nsight Systems. Events bracket the whole kernel, memory traffic included."},
 {id:"p51m6q5", q:"Inside WSL2 Ubuntu on your laptop you ran sudo apt install cuda, and the GPU setup broke. According to the lesson, what went wrong?", options:["The cuda package adds a Linux driver WSL must not have; use cuda-toolkit","WSL2 cannot reach NVIDIA GPUs at all, so use Colab's free T4 instead","CUDA 13 dropped Blackwell support, so you must install CUDA 12 instead","The Linux driver has to be installed inside WSL first, then the toolkit"], answer:0, why:"In WSL2 the GPU appears through the Windows driver, so inside Ubuntu you install only the toolkit package; cuda and cuda-drivers try to add a Linux driver that conflicts with it. Installing a separate driver first is the native-Linux procedure, not the WSL one."}
],
"p51m7":[
 {id:"p51m7q1", q:"You fully fine-tune a 13B-parameter model with mixed-precision Adam, sharding weights, gradients and optimizer state across 8 GPUs with FSDP. About how much memory per GPU do those take, before activations?", options:["About 208 GB, since each GPU holds a full copy","About 3.3 GB, one-eighth of the 26 GB of BF16 weights","About 52 GB, at 4 bytes per parameter per GPU","About 26 GB"], answer:3, why:"Mixed-precision Adam keeps 16 bytes per parameter (2 weights, 2 gradients, 4 master weights, 4 + 4 moments): 13 × 10⁹ × 16 = 208 GB. Sharding across 8 GPUs brings that toward 208 ÷ 8 = 26 GB each. Plain data parallelism would need all 208 GB on every GPU."},
 {id:"p51m7q2", q:"Training a 7B-parameter model, each GPU processes 2,500 tokens per second against a dense BF16 peak of 1,000 TFLOP/s. Taking about 6 × parameters FLOPs per token, what is the model FLOPs utilization?", options:["About 1.8%","About 10.5%","About 3.5%","About 21%"], answer:1, why:"Useful work = 6 × 7 × 10⁹ × 2,500 = 1.05 × 10¹⁴ FLOP/s, 105 TFLOP/s, which is 10.5% of 1,000 TFLOP/s. Using 2 × parameters gives 3.5%, but that counts only the forward pass; training also runs the backward pass, about twice as many FLOPs again."},
 {id:"p51m7q3", q:"GCC at -O3 -march=native will not vectorize for (i = 0; i < n; i++) s += a[i] * b[i]; on floats, and -fopt-info-vec-missed blames the reduction. What is the safest fix?", options:["Add restrict to a and b so the compiler can rule out aliasing","Build the whole program with -ffast-math to allow reordering","Add #pragma omp simd reduction(+:s) to allow it for this loop","Enable AVX-512 with -mavx512f, as wider vectors fix reductions"], answer:2, why:"Strict IEEE rules forbid reordering FP additions, which a vectorized sum requires. The simd reduction pragma grants that permission for this one loop; -ffast-math also works but relaxes FP semantics everywhere. Nothing is stored, so aliasing is not the issue, and HX laptop chips lack AVX-512."},
 {id:"p51m7q4", q:"An 8B model at 4-bit (about 4.5 GB) decodes on the RTX 5090 Laptop GPU (896 GB/s) with a 64K-token context whose FP16 KV cache is 8 GiB, about 8.6 GB. What is the single-stream decode ceiling?", options:["About 68 tokens/s","About 199 tokens/s","About 104 tokens/s","About 34 tokens/s"], answer:0, why:"Each token reads all the weights plus the whole KV cache: 4.5 + 8.6 ≈ 13.1 GB, so 896 ÷ 13.1 ≈ 68 tokens/s. 199 tokens/s ignores the cache, which at long context outweighs the weights; quantizing the KV cache would raise the ceiling."},
 {id:"p51m7q5", q:"Why do modern training runs compute their matrix multiplies in BF16 rather than FP16?", options:["BF16 has more mantissa bits, so each product is more precise","FP16 cannot run on tensor cores, whereas BF16 can","BF16 takes half the memory of FP16, doubling throughput","BF16 keeps FP32's exponent range, so no loss scaling is needed"], answer:3, why:"BF16 has FP32's 8 exponent bits, so gradients neither underflow nor overflow; FP16, with 5 exponent bits and a maximum of 65,504, needs loss scaling. BF16 actually has fewer mantissa bits than FP16 (7 versus 10), and both use 2 bytes and run on tensor cores."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p52m1":[
 {id:"p52m1q1", q:"A motor driver switches at 16.05 kHz, and its ripple reaches a current sensor sampled at 2 kHz with no analog filter. Where does the ripple appear in your data, and what removes it?", options:["At 50 Hz; add an analog low-pass or sample in sync with the PWM","Nowhere; energy above the 1 kHz Nyquist frequency is discarded","At 50 Hz; a digital low-pass after the ADC cleans it up later","At 14.05 kHz; it is above hearing, so the controller ignores it"], answer:0, why:"Subtract the nearest multiple of fs: |16.05 − 8 × 2| = 0.05 kHz, a phantom 50 Hz signal inside the controller's band. Once sampled it is indistinguishable from real 50 Hz motion, so it must be stopped before the converter or caught at the same PWM phase every sample."},
 {id:"p52m1q2", q:"A 12-bit ADC spans 0 to 3.3 V. For an ideal converter on a busy signal, what are its step size and its rms quantization noise?", options:["0.81 mV step, about 0.23 mV rms","0.81 mV step, about 0.40 mV rms","3.2 mV step, about 0.93 mV rms","1.6 mV step, about 0.47 mV rms"], answer:0, why:"Δ = FSR/2^N = 3.3 V ÷ 4,096 = 0.806 mV, and uniformly distributed rounding error has an rms of Δ/√12 = 0.806 ÷ 3.46 ≈ 0.23 mV. 0.40 mV is the peak error, Δ/2, and 3.2 mV is the step of a 10-bit converter."},
 {id:"p52m1q3", q:"An ADC samples a 10 MHz input with 2 ps rms of clock jitter. Whatever its bit depth, roughly what SNR is it limited to?", options:["About 98 dB, the 16-bit ideal","About 78 dB","About 58 dB","About 44 dB"], answer:1, why:"The jitter ceiling is SNR = −20 log10(2π·f·tj) = −20 log10(2π × 10⁷ × 2 × 10⁻¹²) = −20 log10(1.26 × 10⁻⁴) ≈ 78 dB, about 12.7 effective bits. A 16-bit part cannot reach its 98 dB ideal at this frequency unless the clock improves."},
 {id:"p52m1q4", q:"Speech recorded on a 16-bit interface peaks at −24 dBFS. Roughly how much resolution is going unused, and what should you change?", options:["About 4 bits; normalize in software afterwards to win them back","About 24 bits; nothing at all can be recovered from it","None; a 16-bit file always carries 16 bits of resolution","About 4 bits; raise the input gain so peaks sit near −12 dBFS"], answer:3, why:"Each bit is worth 6.02 dB, so 24 dB of unused range is about 4 bits. Normalizing later scales the noise and the coarse steps up along with the speech, so it recovers nothing; raising the analog gain before the ADC does, and −12 dBFS peaks still leave headroom."},
 {id:"p52m1q5", q:"A motor speed loop needs a closed-loop bandwidth of 25 Hz. Which sampling rate fits the lesson's guidance for control loops?", options:["About 51 Hz, just above twice the bandwidth","About 20 kHz, to match the PWM frequency","About 500 Hz, 10–30 times the bandwidth","About 25 Hz, one sample per cycle of bandwidth"], answer:2, why:"A controller must see and react to the signal's shape now, not reconstruct it later, and every sample of delay costs phase margin; 10–30× the closed-loop bandwidth, 250–750 Hz, keeps that cost small. Twice the bandwidth is the sampling theorem's reconstruction floor, not a control rule."}
],
"p52m2":[
 {id:"p52m2q1", q:"At fs = 8 kHz you need to separate two tones that are 0.5 Hz apart. What does that require?", options:["An FFT of at least 16,000 points, zero padding as needed","A Blackman window, whose narrow main lobe separates them","At least 2 s of real signal, 16,000 samples","At least 0.5 s of real signal, 4,000 samples"], answer:2, why:"Resolution is Δf = 1/record length, so 0.5 Hz needs at least 2 s of data, 16,000 samples at 8 kHz. Zero padding only interpolates between bins, and windows widen the main lobe (Blackman's spans 6 bins), so neither helps; resolution is bought with time."},
 {id:"p52m2q2", q:"You calibrate a sensor by reading the exact amplitude of a test tone whose frequency may fall anywhere between two FFT bins. Which window does the lesson recommend?", options:["Flat top, for its roughly 0.01 dB scalloping loss","Rectangular, since it does not reduce the gain","Hann, because it is the general-purpose default","Blackman, for its −58 dB highest side lobe"], answer:0, why:"Scalloping loss is how much a tone's peak drops halfway between bins: 3.9 dB rectangular, 1.4 dB Hann, 1.1 dB Blackman, about 0.01 dB flat top. For amplitude accuracy you accept the flat top's wide main lobe. Rectangular loses no gain but has the worst scalloping."},
 {id:"p52m2q3", q:"A tone sitting exactly on a bin is analyzed with N = 8,192 samples and a Hann window. The peak magnitude |X[k]| is 1,024. What is the tone's amplitude?", options:["0.25","0.125","1,024","0.5"], answer:3, why:"For a windowed FFT, amplitude = 2|X[k]|/Σw, and a Hann window sums to about N/2 = 4,096, so 2 × 1,024 ÷ 4,096 = 0.5. Dividing by N instead, which gives 0.25, ignores the window's average gain of 0.5."},
 {id:"p52m2q4", q:"You lengthen an FFT of the same white-noise signal from 4,096 to 65,536 points. What happens to the displayed noise floor per bin?", options:["It stays put, since the noise power did not change","It drops about 12 dB, as the noise spreads over 16× more bins","It drops about 24 dB, since 20 log10(16) applies here","It rises about 12 dB, as the extra samples add more noise"], answer:1, why:"Total noise power is fixed, and a 16× longer FFT spreads it over 16× more bins, so each bin holds 10 log10(16) ≈ 12 dB less: processing gain. Power ratios use 10 log10, not 20. A PSD in V²/Hz, by contrast, does not change with N."},
 {id:"p52m2q5", q:"A sensor's spectrum shows a strong family of lines at 120, 240 and 360 Hz, with little at 60 Hz itself. Following the lesson, what is the most likely source?", options:["Rectifier ripple from a power supply","A ground loop or transformer coupling","A 20 kHz PWM aliasing into the band","Imbalance in a motor turning at 60 Hz"], answer:0, why:"Full-wave rectification produces ripple at twice the mains frequency, so a strong 120 Hz family points at a power supply. Transformers, motors and ground loops show a dominant 60 Hz line with an odd series at 180 and 300 Hz, and motor imbalance appears at the rotation rate itself."}
],
"p52m3":[
 {id:"p52m3q1", q:"Compute the convolution y = h * x for x = [2, 1, 3] and h = [1, −1].", options:["[2, −1, 2]","[2, −1, 2, −3]","[−2, 1, −2, 3]","[2, 3, 4, 3]"], answer:1, why:"y[n] = Σ h[k]·x[n − k]: y[0] = 2, y[1] = 1 − 2 = −1, y[2] = 3 − 1 = 2, y[3] = −3. The output has length 3 + 2 − 1 = 4, so stopping at three samples drops the tail; [2, 3, 4, 3] is what h = [1, 1] would give."},
 {id:"p52m3q2", q:"A sensor is sampled at 2.4 kHz and picks up 60 Hz hum with its harmonics. Which moving average removes all of them, and how much delay does it add?", options:["M = 40, adding about 8.1 ms of delay","M = 60, adding about 12.3 ms of delay","M = 40, adding no delay as it is an FIR","M = 20, adding about 4.0 ms of delay"], answer:0, why:"An M-point average has nulls at every multiple of fs/M, so M = 2,400/60 = 40 kills 60 Hz and all its harmonics. Its delay is (M − 1)/2 = 19.5 samples, 8.1 ms. M = 20 nulls only multiples of 120 Hz and misses 60 and 180 Hz."},
 {id:"p52m3q3", q:"What kind of filter is y[n] = x[n] − x[n−1]?", options:["A low-pass: gain 1 at DC and 0 at Nyquist","Unstable: it has a pole on the unit circle at z = 1","A high-pass: gain 0 at DC and 2 at Nyquist","An all-pass: gain 1 at every frequency"], answer:2, why:"H(z) = 1 − z⁻¹, so the DC gain H(1) = 0 and the Nyquist gain H(−1) = 2: a differencer, which is high-pass. Its root at z = 1 is a zero, not a pole; with no feedback it is an FIR filter and therefore always stable."},
 {id:"p52m3q4", q:"An exponential moving average y[n] = y[n−1] + 0.1·(x[n] − y[n−1]) runs at fs = 400 Hz. Where is its pole, and roughly where is its −3 dB point?", options:["Pole at z = 0.1; −3 dB near 40 Hz","Pole at z = 0.9; −3 dB near 40 Hz","Pole at z = 1.1; the filter is unstable","Pole at z = 0.9; −3 dB near 7 Hz"], answer:3, why:"Rewritten, y[n] = 0.1x[n] + 0.9y[n−1], so H(z) = 0.1/(1 − 0.9z⁻¹): a pole at 0.9, inside the circle and near z = 1, so low-pass. Its RC equivalent is T(1 − α)/α = 0.0225 s, a −3 dB point near 1/(2π × 0.0225) ≈ 7 Hz; 40 Hz is just α × fs."},
 {id:"p52m3q5", q:"A distance sensor feeds a 200 Hz position loop that needs about 10 Hz of bandwidth. The signal carries 50 Hz interference plus white noise. Which filter plan suits the loop?", options:["sosfiltfilt with a 4th-order Butterworth, for zero phase shift","A 50 Hz notch plus a 2nd-order low-pass well above 10 Hz","An 8th-order Butterworth at 12 Hz in b, a form, for a sharp edge","A 101-tap linear-phase FIR at 15 Hz, which adds no delay"], answer:1, why:"Every filter's delay subtracts phase margin, so control loops notch specific interference and use a low-order low-pass well above the loop bandwidth. sosfiltfilt needs future samples, a sharp 8th-order filter adds delay and is fragile in b, a form, and a 101-tap FIR delays 50 samples, 250 ms."}
],
"p52m4":[
 {id:"p52m4q1", q:"A unity-feedback loop has loop gain L = 50. The plant then loses 30% of its gain, so L becomes 35. By roughly how much does the closed-loop gain T change?", options:["About 30%, the same as the plant","Less than 1%","About 15%, half the plant's change","Not at all; feedback removes all sensitivity"], answer:1, why:"T = L/(1 + L) moves from 50/51 = 0.980 to 35/36 = 0.972, a change of about 0.8%. Feedback divides the sensitivity to plant changes by about 1 + L. It shrinks the effect without removing it, and it passes sensor errors straight through."},
 {id:"p52m4q2", q:"A heater at 0% power sits at 20 °C. You step it to 50%; the temperature settles at 120 °C and passes 83.2 °C at 30 s. What are K and τ, and the 10–90% rise time?", options:["K = 2.4 °C per %, τ = 30 s, rise about 66 s","K = 2 °C per %, τ = 30 s, rise about 120 s","K = 2 °C per %, τ = 63 s, rise about 139 s","K = 2 °C per %, τ = 30 s, rise about 66 s"], answer:3, why:"K is the change in output over the change in input: 100 °C ÷ 50% = 2 °C per %. 83.2 °C is 63.2% of the 100 °C rise, so τ = 30 s, and the 10–90% rise time is τ ln 9 ≈ 2.2τ = 66 s. 120 s is the 2% settling time, 4τ."},
 {id:"p52m4q3", q:"A mass–spring–damper has m = 0.5 kg, k = 200 N/m and c = 14 N·s/m. What step response should you predict?", options:["ζ = 0.7: about 4.6% overshoot, settling in about 0.29 s","ζ = 0.35: about 31% overshoot, settling in about 0.57 s","ζ = 0.7: about 4.6% overshoot, settling in about 0.57 s","ζ = 1.4: no overshoot at all, and a sluggish response"], answer:0, why:"ωn = √(k/m) = √400 = 20 rad/s and ζ = c/(2√(km)) = 14/(2 × 10) = 0.7, giving about 4.6% overshoot and ts ≈ 4/(ζωn) = 4/14 ≈ 0.29 s. Overshoot depends on ζ alone; ωn only sets the time scale."},
 {id:"p52m4q4", q:"A loop crosses over at 20 rad/s with 50° of phase margin. You move its controller to a laptop that adds 30 ms of delay. What phase margin remains?", options:["Still 50°, since a delay changes no gain","About 48°, as 30 ms is only 0.03 rad","About 16°, as the delay costs about 34°","None, since any delay destabilizes feedback"], answer:2, why:"A pure delay costs ω·Td of phase without changing gain: 20 × 0.030 = 0.6 rad ≈ 34° at crossover, leaving about 16°, far below the 45–60° target, so the response will ring badly. Forgetting to multiply by ω gives the tempting 0.03 rad."},
 {id:"p52m4q5", q:"A system ẋ = Ax has A = [[0, 1], [−4, −1]]. What do its poles say about its behavior?", options:["Poles −1 and −4: stable with no oscillation","Poles +0.5 ± j1.94: a growing oscillation","Poles 0 and −1: marginally stable, drifting","Poles −0.5 ± j1.94: stable but lightly damped"], answer:3, why:"The poles are the eigenvalues of A: det(sI − A) = s² + s + 4 = 0 gives s = −0.5 ± j1.94. Negative real parts mean stable; ωn = 2 rad/s and ζ = 1/(2 × 2) = 0.25 mean it rings with about 44% overshoot. −1 and −4 are just matrix entries."}
],
"p52m5":[
 {id:"p52m5q1", q:"A PID loop runs at T = 5 ms with Kd = 0.1 and takes the derivative of the error. The setpoint steps by 20 units. What happens on that sample, and what is the cure?", options:["A 0.01-unit blip, far too small to matter","A 400-unit spike; take D on the measurement","A 2-unit nudge; add more integral action","A 400-unit spike; raise the filter's N to 50"], answer:1, why:"The error jumps 20 units in one sample, so Kd·Δe/T = 0.1 × 20 ÷ 0.005 = 400 units, slamming the actuator. Using −dy/dt instead behaves identically between setpoint changes and never kicks. A larger N weakens the derivative filter, so noise gets worse and the kick stays."},
 {id:"p52m5q2", q:"After tuning, a level loop shows a slow, rolling oscillation with large overshoot, although the offset does go to zero eventually. Which change should you try first?", options:["Reduce Ki, that is, lengthen Ti","Raise Kd until the loop settles","Raise Kp to speed the loop up","Take D on the error instead"], answer:0, why:"A slow, rolling oscillation with big overshoot is the signature of too much integral action, because I acts on accumulated past error and adds phase lag. Reducing Ki calms it. Too much P oscillates fast rather than slowly, and too much D shows up as a jittery, buzzing actuator."},
 {id:"p52m5q3", q:"A step test fits gain K = 2, dead time L = 0.5 s and time constant τ = 5 s. Using SIMC with the default τc = L, what PI settings result?", options:["Kc = 6, Ti = 1 s","Kc = 5, Ti = 4 s","Kc = 2.5, Ti = 5 s","Kc = 2.5, Ti = 4 s"], answer:3, why:"SIMC gives Kc = τ/(K(τc + L)) = 5/(2 × 1.0) = 2.5 and Ti = min(τ, 4(τc + L)) = min(5, 4) = 4 s. Kc = 6 with Ti = 1 s is the more aggressive Ziegler–Nichols reaction-curve result, 1.2τ/(KL) and 2L."},
 {id:"p52m5q4", q:"What is wrong with this control loop running on a PC?\n while True:\n   u = pid.update(r, read_sensor()); write(u)\n   time.sleep(0.01)", options:["time.sleep(0.01) is too precise for a control loop","The PID must be updated after writing u, not before","The period is 10 ms plus compute time, so it drifts","Nothing; sleeping holds the period at exactly 10 ms"], answer:2, why:"Sleeping after the work makes each cycle 10 ms plus however long the work took, so the rate drifts and jitters, and jitter in T becomes noise in the derivative. Schedule against a monotonic clock (next += T, then sleep until next) or use a hardware timer interrupt."},
 {id:"p52m5q5", q:"A robot arm joint sags under gravity after every move while the integrator slowly winds up the holding torque. Which structural change attacks the cause?", options:["Raise Ki until the sag disappears quickly","Add gravity-torque feedforward for the angle","Use derivative on error so it reacts sooner","Lower the loop rate so the integral builds"], answer:1, why:"Feedforward supplies what you already know, the gravity torque at the arm's current angle, so feedback only corrects model error and the integrator has little left to accumulate. Raising Ki instead adds lag and rolling oscillation, and derivative on error adds kick, not holding torque."}
],
"p52m6":[
 {id:"p52m6q1", q:"A robot is at (3.0, −1.0) m in the world, heading 180°. Its lidar sits 0.25 m ahead of base_link and reports an obstacle at (2.0, 0.5) in the lidar frame. Where is the obstacle in the world frame?", options:["(5.25, −0.5)","(1.0, −1.5)","(0.75, −0.5)","(0.75, −1.5)"], answer:3, why:"Add the mount offset first: (2.25, 0.5) in base_link. Rotating by 180° negates both coordinates, giving (−2.25, −0.5), and adding the robot's position gives (0.75, −1.5). Forgetting the 0.25 m mount gives (1.0, −1.5); skipping the rotation gives (5.25, −0.5)."},
 {id:"p52m6q2", q:"A planar arm has L1 = 0.40 m and L2 = 0.25 m. You ask inverse kinematics for the point (0.60, 0.30) m. What comes back?", options:["Two solutions, elbow up and elbow down","No solution: cos θ2 ≈ 1.14, out of reach","One solution, as the point is in quadrant one","Infinitely many, as it sits at a singularity"], answer:1, why:"x² + y² = 0.45, so cos θ2 = (0.45 − 0.16 − 0.0625)/(2 × 0.40 × 0.25) = 1.14, which no angle satisfies. The point is 0.67 m from the shoulder, outside the 0.15–0.65 m workspace ring; checking |cos θ2| ≤ 1 belongs in every IK solver."},
 {id:"p52m6q3", q:"A differential-drive robot with wheel separation b = 0.30 m measures vR = 0.55 m/s and vL = 0.45 m/s. What are its forward speed and turn rate?", options:["v = 0.50 m/s, ω ≈ 0.33 rad/s, turning left","v = 1.00 m/s, ω ≈ 0.33 rad/s, turning left","v = 0.50 m/s, ω ≈ 0.03 rad/s, turning left","v = 0.50 m/s, ω ≈ 0.33 rad/s, turning right"], answer:0, why:"v = (vR + vL)/2 = 0.50 m/s and ω = (vR − vL)/b = 0.10/0.30 ≈ 0.33 rad/s. With the right wheel faster, ω is positive, counterclockwise in ROS's x-forward, y-left convention, so the robot turns left. 0.03 rad/s multiplies by b instead of dividing."},
 {id:"p52m6q4", q:"A 1D Kalman filter holds x = 5.0 m with P = 0.5 m². The robot moves +2.0 m with process noise Q = 0.5 m², then a sensor reads z = 7.6 m with R = 1.0 m². What are x and P after the update?", options:["x = 7.6 m, P = 1.0 m²","x = 7.3 m, P = 2.0 m²","x = 7.3 m, P = 0.5 m²","x = 7.2 m, P = 0.33 m²"], answer:2, why:"Predict: x⁻ = 7.0 m and P⁻ = 0.5 + 0.5 = 1.0. Update: K = 1.0/(1.0 + 1.0) = 0.5, x = 7.0 + 0.5 × 0.6 = 7.3 m and P = (1 − 0.5) × 1.0 = 0.5 m². Skipping Q in the prediction gives 7.2 m and an overconfident 0.33."},
 {id:"p52m6q5", q:"With robot_localization fusing wheel odometry and standalone GNSS, the estimated position leaps by a meter or more at every GNSS fix, although the wheels report smooth motion. Which tuning error fits best?", options:["The odometry Q is set far too small","The GNSS R is set far too small","The filter runs at too high a rate","The odometry R is set far too large"], answer:1, why:"Standalone GNSS is good to only 2–5 m, so its R should be several square meters. Declaring it tiny tells the filter every fix is nearly perfect, so the gain approaches 1 and the estimate leaps to each noisy fix. A too-small odometry Q would make the output smoother, not jumpier."}
],
"p52m7":[
 {id:"p52m7q1", q:"A node subscribed to /scan receives nothing. ros2 topic info /scan -v shows the lidar driver publishing BEST_EFFORT and your subscriber requesting RELIABLE. What is the fix?", options:["Give both nodes the same ROS_DOMAIN_ID","Set the subscriber's QoS to best effort","Fetch each scan through a service instead","Publish a static transform for the lidar"], answer:1, why:"A reliable subscriber is incompatible with a best-effort publisher, so DDS silently delivers nothing, the lesson's most common empty-topic bug. Sensor streams should be best effort, so match the subscriber to it. The nodes already discover each other, or topic info would not list both."},
 {id:"p52m7q2", q:"On an 8-connected grid where straight moves cost 1 and diagonals √2, A* plans from cell (2, 3) to cell (9, 7). What is the octile heuristic, and would Manhattan distance keep A* optimal?", options:["8.66; Manhattan's 11 is also admissible, just slower","11; Manhattan distance is the exact cost on any grid","8.66; Manhattan's 11 overestimates, so it may not","8.06; octile is the same as straight-line distance"], answer:2, why:"Octile = Δmax + (√2 − 1)·Δmin = 7 + 0.414 × 4 ≈ 8.66, the true cost with no obstacles. Manhattan's 11 ignores diagonal moves, so on an 8-connected grid it can overestimate and A* may return a longer path. Straight-line distance, 8.06, is admissible but looser."},
 {id:"p52m7q3", q:"On a map where the optimal path is 30 m, you switch the planner to weighted A* with w = 1.5. What does theory guarantee about the path it returns?", options:["It is exactly 30 m; weighting changes only speed","It is at most 31.5 m, as weighting adds 5%","It is at least 45 m, since the cost is inflated","It is at most 45 m, usually with fewer expansions"], answer:3, why:"Multiplying an admissible heuristic by w > 1 makes A* greedier and faster but only bounds the result: up to w × optimal, here 45 m. It may still return 30 m, as in the lesson's warehouse example, but that is luck rather than a guarantee."},
 {id:"p52m7q4", q:"In simulation, obstacles land in the right place while the robot is still or driving straight, but smear sideways whenever it turns. What is the most likely bug?", options:["Scans are transformed with the latest pose, not their stamp","A QoS mismatch is quietly dropping half of the scans","The lidar's static transform has the wrong mounting height","The map frame jumped once after a loop closure"], answer:0, why:"A scan taken 50 ms ago must be transformed with where the robot was 50 ms ago. Using the newest transform barely matters at constant heading but rotates points wrongly during turns. A wrong mount would be wrong all the time, and a loop-closure jump is a one-off shift."},
 {id:"p52m7q5", q:"Your mobile robot receives /cmd_vel from a laptop over Wi-Fi. Which setup follows the lesson's bench safety rules?", options:["A stop button in RViz, with speed limits enforced inside the planner","A cmd_vel watchdog alone, since Wi-Fi dropouts are the real hazard","Hardwired e-stop on motor power, cmd_vel watchdog, wheels up first","SROS2 encryption on every topic, so nobody else can command it"], answer:2, why:"Software fails exactly when it is needed, so the emergency stop must cut actuator power in hardware; a watchdog that zeroes velocity when /cmd_vel goes silent and first runs with the wheels off the ground are added layers. SROS2 is a security control, not a safety stop."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p53m1":[
 {id:"p53m1q1", q:"Production units of your gateway ship with an unlocked UART console that drops straight to a root shell. Scoring the path: under a day (0), proficient (3), public knowledge (0), easy window (1), standard equipment (0). What is the rating, and the fitting control?", options:["4, Basic; fuse-lock or authenticate debug on every production unit","4, Basic; acceptable, because an attacker needs physical access first","14, Moderate; a tamper-evident label over the header is enough","4, High; only a lab-class attacker with bespoke tools could do it"], answer:0, why:"0 + 3 + 0 + 1 + 0 = 4 points, Basic: within reach of almost anyone with a screwdriver and a USB-serial adapter. This is CWE-1191, a debug interface without access control, and the control is fuse-locked or authenticated debug. Physical access is cheap, not a nation-state barrier."},
 {id:"p53m1q2", q:"A design review pairs each trust-boundary crossing on a laptop board with a control. Which pairing is correct?", options:["Memory bus: a pre-boot PIN that BitLocker asks for at every start","SPI flash: hardware-verified boot plus flash write protection","Thunderbolt DMA: memory encryption in the memory controller","Debug header: an IOMMU switched on before the OS starts"], answer:1, why:"Whoever can write SPI flash owns every boot, so the image must be verified by a hardware root (Boot Guard or Platform Secure Boot) and the flash write-protected. DMA is stopped by an IOMMU, the memory bus by memory encryption, and debug headers by fuse-locked or authenticated debug."},
 {id:"p53m1q3", q:"Researchers report an exploitable bug in the boot ROM of the SoC used in a product you have already shipped. What is the realistic engineering response?", options:["Push a signed fwupd capsule that rewrites the boot ROM in the field","Add the boot ROM's hash to dbx so firmware refuses to run it","Protect data with keys the bug cannot reach; fix it in new silicon","Tell customers to reinstall the OS, which clears the vulnerable code"], answer:2, why:"Boot ROM is immutable, so no update can fix it; checkm8 on Apple's A5–A11 chips is the model. The response is architectural: keep secrets behind keys the ROM bug cannot reach, detect abuse where possible, and correct the ROM in the next silicon revision. dbx governs UEFI images, not mask ROM."},
 {id:"p53m1q4", q:"After a board maker's firmware signing keys leak in a breach, your company reviews how its own firmware signing keys are protected. Which control addresses that stage of the supply chain?", options:["Publish a firmware SBOM with every release","Buy chips only through authorized distribution","Turn on Kernel DMA Protection on build servers","Hold the keys in an HSM with M-of-N key ceremonies"], answer:3, why:"A leaked signing key lets anyone produce firmware that verified boot accepts, so signing keys belong in HSMs with key ceremonies, M-of-N quorum and logged use. SBOMs answer which components are inside an image, and authorized distribution addresses counterfeit parts; neither protects a key."},
 {id:"p53m1q5", q:"A desktop keeps beaconing to an unknown host after a full disk wipe and a clean OS reinstall. Which response follows the lesson?", options:["Wipe and reinstall again, this time from a different USB stick","Suspect firmware: reflash from a trusted source, then check measured boot","Replace the SSD, since only the drive itself can hold the infection","Trust it now, since the fresh OS can confirm its firmware is clean"], answer:1, why:"Implants in SPI flash, option ROMs or device controllers survive a disk wipe, so reflash firmware from a trusted source and use verified boot plus measured boot and attestation to confirm. Asking the running system is useless: a component cannot vouch for itself, and compromised firmware can report anything."}
],
"p53m2":[
 {id:"p53m2q1", q:"On a dual-boot PC, the PowerShell check of the Secure Boot PK for 'DO NOT TRUST|DO NOT SHIP' returns True. What does that mean, and what is the fix?", options:["The 2023 Microsoft certificates are missing; apply the update","Secure Boot is disabled; switch it back on in firmware setup","The dbx is out of date; refresh it with fwupd and reboot","The PK is a public test key; install OEM firmware with a real PK"], answer:3, why:"That string marks a test platform key whose private key is public (PKfail), so anyone could sign a new KEK and take over Secure Boot policy. The fix is OEM firmware that installs a genuine PK. Missing 2023 certificates show up in the db check, not the PK check."},
 {id:"p53m2q2", q:"A fleet never received Microsoft's 2023 Secure Boot certificates. What happens to those PCs after the 2011 certificates expire in 2026?", options:["They keep booting but get no new boot managers or revocations","They stop booting, because firmware enforces certificate expiry","Secure Boot drops into setup mode and stops enforcing anything","BitLocker asks every one of them for its recovery key"], answer:0, why:"Firmware has no trusted clock and does not enforce expiry when it checks a boot image, so the machines boot normally. They are frozen, though: new boot managers and db and dbx updates signed under the 2023 CAs never apply, a silent loss of protection."},
 {id:"p53m2q3", q:"Which design for an embedded device's firmware update matches the lesson's checklist?", options:["Check the signature only, since a valid signature proves the image is current","Check a SHA-256 hash that ships inside the same update package as the image","Check signature and SVN, write the inactive slot, boot it under a watchdog","Write over the running image to save flash space, then verify it on next boot"], answer:2, why:"A signature proves origin, not freshness: without a security version check an old, signed, vulnerable image installs, as BlackLotus showed for boot managers. A/B slots with a watchdog and automatic fallback keep a failed update from bricking the device; a hash shipped beside the image proves nothing."},
 {id:"p53m2q4", q:"Secure Boot is on, yet a bootkit that relies on an old, validly signed boot manager persisted across reboots. Which pair of controls prevents it and detects it?", options:["Add the bootkit's hash to db, then reinstall Windows from clean media","Revoke it in dbx, raise the SVN, and attest measured boot remotely","Switch Secure Boot off and on again to reset all of its databases","Enable DRTM so that the old boot manager is skipped on every boot"], answer:1, why:"Verified boot runs anything validly signed, so the cure for a rollback is revocation: dbx entries, security version numbers and trust moved to the 2023 CA. Measured boot records exactly which manager ran, and a remote verifier rejecting it detects the bootkit. Adding hashes to db would allow, not block."},
 {id:"p53m2q5", q:"A DICE-based microcontroller receives a firmware update. What happens to its compound device identifier (CDI), and why is that useful?", options:["It stays the same, since the device secret in fuses is fixed","The fused device secret is rewritten to match the new firmware","It changes only after a factory reset clears the old firmware","It changes, so keys derived from it attest to the running firmware"], answer:3, why:"The CDI is KDF(unique device secret, hash of the first mutable code), and each later stage derives its secret the same way. New firmware yields a new CDI and new keys, so a key that signs an attestation is itself evidence of the firmware holding it. The fused secret never changes and is locked after reset."}
],
"p53m3":[
 {id:"p53m3q1", q:"A Linux laptop's LUKS2 key is sealed to PCRs 0+2+4+7, and every firmware update or GPU swap forces recovery. The threat is theft from a vehicle. Which re-enrollment fits?", options:["Seal to no PCRs, so the TPM releases the key on every boot","Seal to PCR 7 with a TPM PIN; escrow the recovery key elsewhere","Seal to PCR 0 alone, as firmware is the main thing that changes","Seal to PCR 16, which the OS can reset whenever it applies updates"], answer:1, why:"PCR 7 trusts the Secure Boot policy rather than exact firmware and option-ROM hashes, so routine updates stop triggering recovery, and a PIN guarded by the TPM's lockout covers theft. Sealing to nothing hands the key to a tampered bootloader; PCR 16 is resettable by software and proves nothing."},
 {id:"p53m3q2", q:"A verifier replays a device's event log for PCR 4, and the result does not match the PCR 4 value in the signed quote. What should it conclude?", options:["The log was altered or is incomplete; reject the evidence","The log is unprotected, so small mismatches are normal noise","The TPM is faulty, so trust the log's descriptions instead","Only SHA-1 logs can be replayed; retry with the SHA-1 bank"], answer:0, why:"Replaying the log must reproduce the quoted PCR exactly; a mismatch means entries were edited, removed or added, or something was measured without being logged. The log needs no protection precisely because this check exposes tampering, so the verifier rejects the evidence."},
 {id:"p53m3q3", q:"A PC whose BitLocker uses the older PCR profile 0, 2, 4 and 11 asks for its recovery key right after you install a new graphics card. Which PCR most likely changed?", options:["PCR 7, the Secure Boot state","PCR 11, BitLocker's own access control","PCR 4, the boot manager code","PCR 2, option ROM code"], answer:3, why:"Firmware executes the new card's option ROM and measures it into PCR 2, so the sealed value no longer matches. The PCR 7 profile usually avoids this by trusting the Secure Boot policy instead, and suspending BitLocker for one reboot before a hardware change avoids the prompt."},
 {id:"p53m3q4", q:"At enrollment, how does a verifier establish that a new attestation key (AK) lives in the same genuine TPM as an EK whose certificate chains to the manufacturer?", options:["The AK signs its own certificate, which the verifier then pins","The EK co-signs every quote together with the AK","It runs MakeCredential; only that TPM can ActivateCredential","The event log records the AK's persistent handle"], answer:2, why:"MakeCredential encrypts a challenge to the EK, bound to the AK's name; only the TPM holding that EK, with that AK loaded, can complete ActivateCredential and return it. The EK never signs anything itself, because it identifies the machine; the restricted AK signs quotes."},
 {id:"p53m3q5", q:"A server passes Keylime boot attestation every hour, yet an attacker is running a web shell inside a service process that touched no measured file. What does this show?", options:["Keylime is misconfigured, since attestation detects any compromise","Boot attestation proves only what was measured; add IMA policy and EDR","The attacker must have forged the quote with the endorsement key","The nonce check failed, so an old good quote was being replayed"], answer:1, why:"Attestation proves what was measured, nothing more. A runtime compromise that changes no measured component is invisible to boot measurements, so runtime file measurement through IMA, a strict appraisal policy and EDR still matter. A forged quote would need the AK; the EK signs nothing."}
],
"p53m4":[
 {id:"p53m4q1", q:"Your key service verifies an SEV-SNP report: the VCEK chains to AMD's ARK, the launch measurement equals your CI value, the debug bit is clear and the report data matches the presented key. Which check is still missing before release?", options:["The guest's IP address is on the allowlist","The host's discrete TPM endorsement certificate","A hypervisor signature over the measurement","The TCB version is at or above your minimum"], answer:3, why:"A genuine chip running old, vulnerable firmware or microcode still produces a validly signed report, so the verifier must also require a minimum TCB version; that is how SGX-era flaws were handled through TCB recovery. The hypervisor is untrusted, so its signature would add nothing."},
 {id:"p53m4q2", q:"Why does the guest place SHA-256 of a freshly generated public key in the 64-byte report data before requesting its SNP report?", options:["It ties the report to a key only this VM holds, for wrapping secrets","It encrypts the report so the hypervisor cannot read the measurement","It lets AMD's key distribution service identify the cloud customer","It stands in for the launch measurement when the firmware changes"], answer:0, why:"The report data binds the evidence to this session: the key service wraps the disk or model key to the public key whose hash sits in the signed report, so only the VM holding the private key can unwrap it. Without that binding, a valid report could be paired with someone else's key."},
 {id:"p53m4q3", q:"A team wants to move an existing Linux database VM to cloud hardware without trusting the hypervisor and without rewriting the application. Which isolation model fits?", options:["An SGX enclave around the database process","A confidential VM on SEV-SNP or Intel TDX","A TrustZone trusted app in the secure world","A separate Secure Enclave-style coprocessor"], answer:1, why:"A confidential VM protects a whole guest from the hypervisor, so an existing VM can be lifted and shifted. SGX requires partitioning the application into an enclave, TrustZone hosts small trusted apps beside a phone or embedded OS, and a coprocessor holds a few keys, not a database."},
 {id:"p53m4q4", q:"Inside that SEV-SNP VM, the database writes to a virtual disk and serves clients over the network. What must the guest itself do?", options:["Nothing; memory encryption also covers disk and network traffic","Ask the host to encrypt the virtual disk on the host side","Encrypt the disk with dm-crypt and the network with TLS","Register the disk in the reverse map table for integrity"], answer:2, why:"Devices are outside the boundary: I/O passes through shared, unencrypted bounce buffers that the host can read. The guest must encrypt what it sends out, with TLS and dm-crypt and keys the host never holds; asking the untrusted host to encrypt defeats the purpose. The RMP covers memory pages, not devices."},
 {id:"p53m4q5", q:"A confidential VM passes full attestation. Which of these does the host still control?", options:["Whether the VM runs at all: it can pause, starve or kill it","Reading guest memory in plaintext through the hypervisor","Silently remapping guest pages to replay older contents","Reading guest registers in plaintext on every VM exit"], answer:0, why:"The host schedules the VM, so availability is never protected. Memory encryption keeps plaintext from the hypervisor, SNP's reverse map table stops silent remapping and replay, and registers are saved encrypted on each exit. Side channels and physical interposers remain further residual risks."}
],
"p53m5":[
 {id:"p53m5q1", q:"A service makes 500,000 system calls per second per core, and on its older CPU the kernel-entry mitigations add about 80 ns per round trip. What do they cost?", options:["About 0.04% of a core","About 4% of a core","About 40% of a core","About 0.4% of a core"], answer:1, why:"500,000 × 80 ns = 40 ms of every second, 4% of a core. That measured, workload-specific figure is what to present; if it is too high, the answer is newer hardware with PCID and enhanced IBRS, not mitigations=off on a production server."},
 {id:"p53m5q2", q:"What is the security problem with this MAC check, and what is the fix?\n for (i = 0; i < 32; i++) if (tag[i] != expected[i]) return 0;\n return 1;", options:["None; every byte is compared before it can return 1","It leaks only if the length 32 is secret; pass n instead","Its run time reveals the matching prefix; use CRYPTO_memcmp","Declaring it inline makes the compiler remove the branch"], answer:2, why:"Returning at the first mismatch makes the run time depend on how many leading bytes matched. Accumulate differences over every byte, or better, use a maintained primitive such as CRYPTO_memcmp or hmac.compare_digest, and check the compiled code at the optimization level you ship."},
 {id:"p53m5q3", q:"A code review finds out = sbox[key[i] ^ in[i]]; inside a table-based cipher. Why is it flagged, and what replaces it?", options:["XOR has secret-dependent timing on most CPUs; use addition instead","The index depends on the key, so cache timing varies; use AES-NI","The array could overflow; add a bounds check before the lookup","Lookups are slow; precompute the table into a larger cache line"], answer:1, why:"A memory index computed from a secret makes the cache lines touched depend on the key, which is how table-based AES leaked keys. AES-NI or bitsliced code has no secret-dependent addresses. XOR runs in constant time, and a bounds check would add a branch without fixing the index."},
 {id:"p53m5q4", q:"Which mitigation policy matches the lesson for three machines: a host running VMs for untrusted customers, a single-user gaming PC, and an isolated benchmark box that runs nothing untrusted?", options:["Host: defaults; gaming PC: mitigations=off; benchmark box: SMT off","Host: mitigations=off for speed; PC: defaults; benchmark box: full set","All three: mitigations=off, because microcode already fixes the hardware","Host: all plus core scheduling; PC: defaults; benchmark box: off allowed"], answer:3, why:"Untrusted tenants need every mitigation plus core scheduling or SMT off. A gaming PC still runs untrusted JavaScript in its browser, so it keeps the defaults. mitigations=off belongs only on an isolated benchmark machine that runs nothing untrusted, never as a fix for slowness."},
 {id:"p53m5q5", q:"Your timing test of a new compare function reports |t| = 2.1 after 10,000 samples. It ran unpinned, on the powersave governor, with all fixed-class runs first and then all random-class runs. What is the right conclusion?", options:["Inconclusive: rerun pinned, at fixed frequency, with classes interleaved","Pass: a |t| below the 4.5 threshold proves that the code is constant time","Fail: any nonzero t value shows that the function leaks timing information","Pass, as long as the mean difference between classes stays below one nanosecond"], answer:0, why:"Blocked classes let drift in frequency, temperature or background load masquerade as, or hide, a difference, and an unpinned, frequency-scaling CPU adds noise that lowers t. A low t from a poorly controlled run is not evidence: pin the process, fix the governor, interleave classes at random and use enough samples."}
],
"p53m6":[
 {id:"p53m6q1", q:"You need (a) an identity key in each of 10,000 IoT sensors, (b) a code-signing CA key making 5,000 signatures an hour under quorum control, and (c) a sealed disk key on each PC. Which assignment fits?", options:["(a) HSM; (b) TPM; (c) secure element","(a) secure element; (b) HSM; (c) TPM","(a) TPM; (b) secure element; (c) HSM","(a) secure element; (b) TPM; (c) HSM"], answer:1, why:"A secure element costs cents to a few dollars and resists high attack potential, ideal per device. An HSM gives throughput, M-of-N administration and FIPS 140-3 Level 3 for a CA, where a TPM manages only a handful of signatures. The TPM is already in the PC and seals to PCRs."},
 {id:"p53m6q2", q:"A contract requires FIPS 140-3 validated cryptography. A vendor says its VPN 'uses FIPS-approved AES-256 and is FIPS compliant'. What should you ask for?", options:["Its CMVP certificate for that exact module version, and the security policy","Nothing more, because approved algorithms already make the module validated","Its FIPS 140-2 certificate, which still covers new purchases after September 2026","Only the CAVP algorithm certificates, which are sufficient evidence on their own"], answer:0, why:"Validation means a current CMVP certificate for the specific module and version, whose security policy states the boundary, approved algorithms and configuration. 'Compliant' is not an official status, CAVP covers only algorithms, and FIPS 140-2 certificates were due to go Historical on 21 September 2026, ending new federal procurement."},
 {id:"p53m6q3", q:"An evaluation rates a fault path at about three months (10), proficient (3), public knowledge (0), easy window (1) and specialized equipment (4). If an attack along it succeeds, which resistance claims fail?", options:["Only AVA_VAN.2, the lowest level","Only AVA_VAN.5, since 18 points counts as High","AVA_VAN.4 and AVA_VAN.5, but not .2 or .3","Every claim, since any successful attack is a failure"], answer:2, why:"10 + 3 + 0 + 1 + 4 = 18, Moderate attack potential. A product claiming Moderate (AVA_VAN.4) must defeat paths up to 19 and one claiming High (.5) up to 24, so both fail. Basic (.2) and Enhanced-Basic (.3) claims cover paths only up to 9 and 13, so this path was outside their scope."},
 {id:"p53m6q4", q:"A payment terminal's PIN check must survive a single voltage glitch that tries to make it skip one decision. Which countermeasure family is aimed at that?", options:["Masking: split secrets into random shares","Hiding: add noise and random delays","Tamper evidence: void-if-removed seals","Redundancy: check twice, guard control flow"], answer:3, why:"Fault injection aims to make one check give the wrong answer once, so redundancy (compute or check twice and compare, protect control flow and counters) plus glitch detectors defeats it. Masking and hiding target power and EM observation, and a seal only shows that someone opened the case."},
 {id:"p53m6q5", q:"An attacker controls the signing server that calls your FIPS 140-3 Level 3 HSM over PKCS#11. What is the exposure, and what is the response?", options:["None; keys held in an HSM cannot be used from outside it","They can request signatures; review HSM logs, revoke and rotate","Keys were extracted; zeroize every HSM in the fleet at once","No signing is possible without someone present at the HSM"], answer:1, why:"An HSM prevents key extraction, not misuse by an authorized caller, so the attacker can sign or decrypt with the server's credentials. Check the HSM audit log for what was signed, revoke what was misused, rotate credentials and limit usage. Level 3 governs physical tampering, not API calls."}
],
"p53m7":[
 {id:"p53m7q1", q:"A 5,000-device fleet receives an update with a hidden 1% bricking rate. What is the chance that a first ring of 25 devices shows at least one failure, and a second ring of 250?", options:["About 1% and about 10%","About 22% and about 92%","About 25% and 100%","About 78% and about 99%"], answer:1, why:"P(at least one) = 1 − 0.99ⁿ: 1 − 0.99²⁵ ≈ 22% and 1 − 0.99²⁵⁰ ≈ 92%. A tiny first ring catches only common failures, so the second ring is the real gate and its halt rule should fire on a single unexplained brick. 78% is the chance the first ring sees nothing."},
 {id:"p53m7q2", q:"A CVE is announced in an image-decoding library used inside UEFI firmware. What is the fastest way to find your affected images, and to tell customers which builds are unaffected?", options:["Query the firmware SBOMs for that component, then publish VEX statements","Scan each running laptop with an EDR agent, then email every customer","Wait for the BIOS vendor's advisory, then reflash every device just in case","Re-sign every firmware image with a new key so the old ones stop installing"], answer:0, why:"Firmware SBOMs list the modules in each image, so a component query answers 'which images include it' in minutes, as LogoFAIL showed. VEX then states per build whether it is affected or not exploitable, keeping scanners from flooding customers with false alarms. Re-signing changes nothing about the bug."},
 {id:"p53m7q3", q:"An attacker who controls one download mirror serves devices last year's validly signed firmware, which has a known flaw. Which missing control lets it install?", options:["The signatures use RSA rather than a post-quantum scheme like ML-DSA","The images ship without SBOMs, so devices cannot inspect components","Devices lack anti-rollback: an SVN check and TUF-style freshness data","The root key is kept offline rather than inside an online signing service"], answer:2, why:"A replayed old image carries a genuine signature, so signing proves origin but not freshness. The device must refuse images below its stored security version number, and TUF's timestamp and snapshot roles stop a mirror from freezing or rolling back what clients see. An offline root key is correct practice."},
 {id:"p53m7q4", q:"What does this SystemVerilog assertion establish?\n assert property (@(posedge clk) disable iff (!rst_n) lock_q |=> $stable(cfg_q));", options:["Software can never set lock_q, so the register always stays unlocked","That the design is free of every lock-bypass weakness, debug paths included","cfg_q holds steady only in the cycles before lock_q is first set","With lock_q set, cfg_q cannot change; debug-path key leaks are not covered"], answer:3, why:"|=> means that whenever lock_q is high, cfg_q must equal its previous value on the next clock, which addresses CWE-1231, a lock bit that does not really lock. Formal tools prove only the properties written; whether a key can reach a debug port needs a separate information-flow property."},
 {id:"p53m7q5", q:"You find a serious firmware flaw in a router model that you own. Which path is the professional one?", options:["Publish a working proof of concept right away, so the vendor is forced to patch","Check how many neighbors' routers are affected before reporting it","Report privately to the vendor's PSIRT with a timeline, no public exploit","File the Cyber Resilience Act 24-hour early warning with ENISA yourself"], answer:2, why:"Coordinated disclosure means a private report to the vendor's PSIRT (found through security.txt), an agreed timeline and no public exploit, with CERT/CC to help if the vendor does not respond. Testing other people's routers is unauthorized access, and CRA reporting duties fall on manufacturers, not researchers."}
]
});
